# Overview

BitTasker's design system is built on a foundation of **DaisyUI components** with **Tailwind CSS** as the styling engine, ensuring consistency, accessibility, and maintainability across the entire platform. The system prioritizes **DaisyUI components** for all UI elements when feasible, falling back to custom Tailwind classes only when no suitable DaisyUI component exists.

## Design System Philosophy

### Component-First Approach

- **DaisyUI First**: Always use DaisyUI components when available
- **Custom Fallback**: Use Tailwind utilities when DaisyUI doesn't provide a suitable component
- **Consistent Theming**: Global theme settings ensure visual consistency across all components
- **Semantic Colors**: Use semantic color names that automatically adapt to light/dark themes
- **Accessibility**: Built-in accessibility features through DaisyUI's ARIA-compliant components

### Theme Architecture

- **Global Theme Settings**: Centralized theme configuration in Tailwind config
- **Automatic Adaptation**: Components automatically adapt to light/dark mode
- **Bitcoin Branding**: Primary colors reflect Bitcoin orange (#FF9315) theme
- **Semantic Naming**: Use semantic color names (`primary`, `secondary`, `success`, `error`) instead of specific colors

### Development Guidelines

- **Component Consistency**: All similar UI elements use the same DaisyUI components
- **Theme Awareness**: Components automatically respect global theme settings
- **Mobile-First Design**: Prioritize mobile experience as most users will be on mobile devices
- **Responsive Design**: Mobile-first approach with consistent breakpoints
- **Performance**: Optimized bundle size through component reuse and tree shaking

## UI/UX Patterns

## Design System

### Brand Colors

**ALWAYS use DaisyUI color classes instead of custom hex values. DaisyUI provides semantic color names that automatically adapt to themes.**

#### Primary Colors (Use DaisyUI classes)

- **Primary**: `btn-primary`, `text-primary`, `bg-primary` (Bitcoin orange theme)
- **Secondary**: `btn-secondary`, `text-secondary`, `bg-secondary` (Dark theme)
- **Accent**: `btn-accent`, `text-accent`, `bg-accent` (Emerald green)
- **Neutral**: `btn-neutral`, `text-neutral`, `bg-neutral` (Gray)

#### Semantic Colors (Use DaisyUI classes)

- **Success**: `btn-success`, `text-success`, `bg-success`, `alert-success`
- **Warning**: `btn-warning`, `text-warning`, `bg-warning`, `alert-warning`
- **Error**: `btn-error`, `text-error`, `bg-error`, `alert-error`
- **Info**: `btn-info`, `text-info`, `bg-info`, `alert-info`

#### Color Usage Guidelines

- **NEVER use custom hex colors** - always use DaisyUI semantic classes
- **Use semantic color names** - `success`, `error`, `warning`, `info` instead of specific colors
- **Leverage DaisyUI themes** - colors automatically adapt to light/dark modes
- **Use color utilities** - `text-primary`, `bg-secondary`, `border-accent`

### DaisyUI Theme Configuration

**Configure DaisyUI themes in your Tailwind config to use Bitcoin orange as primary:**

```javascript
// tailwind.config.js
module.exports = {
  daisyui: {
    themes: [
      {
        light: {
          "primary": "#FF9315", // Bitcoin orange
          "secondary": "#1f2937", // Dark gray
          "accent": "#10b981", // Emerald green
          "neutral": "#6b7280", // Gray
          "base-100": "#ffffff",
          "base-200": "#f9fafb",
          "base-300": "#f3f4f6",
        },
        dark: {
          "primary": "#FF9315", // Bitcoin orange
          "secondary": "#374151", // Lighter gray for dark mode
          "accent": "#10b981", // Emerald green
          "neutral": "#4b5563", // Lighter gray for dark mode
          "base-100": "#111827",
          "base-200": "#1f2937",
          "base-300": "#374151",
        }
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

### AI Development Guidelines

**When creating UI components, ALWAYS:**

1. **Use DaisyUI components** instead of custom CSS
2. **Prefer semantic color names** (`btn-primary`, `text-success`)
3. **Use DaisyUI component classes** (`btn`, `card`, `modal`, `alert`)
4. **Leverage theme variables** for consistent styling
5. **Test in both light and dark modes** using DaisyUI theme switching
6. **Use `alert-outline` modifier** for all alert components to maintain visual consistency
7. **Avoid using custom CSS unless absolutely necessary** and use tailwind classes for any custom styling needs
8. **Test major UI changes** in the Cursor browser interface, taking screenshots on both desktop and mobile to ensure consistent interface across devices

### Common DaisyUI Color Patterns

```svelte
<!-- Status indicators -->
